    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 251655\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n955 AM PDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Hotter than normal temperatures will continue through the week.\n\n* Best chance of storms is along and south of I-15 today, with \n  diminishing odds afterwards.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the weekend.\n\nHigh pressure is expected to expand back into the region with \nincreasing heights leading to well above normal temperatures\nthrough Friday. This will lead to widespread areas of Major \n(Level 3 of 4) to Extreme (Level 4 of 4) Heat Risk through Friday \nand an Extreme Heat Warning currently in effect. This means highs \nin the upper 100s to mid 110s and lows in the mid 80s to low 90s \nin the valleys across the Mojave Desert including Las Vegas. \nNumerous daily high records and warm minimum temperature records \nare in jeopardy - see the CLIMATE section below for details. An \napproaching trough this weekend will help to bring lowering \nheights allowing temperatures to cool a few degrees by early next \nweek with readings close to normal for this time of year. \n\nPWATs will still remain fairly elevated through mid-week with \nreadings around 1 to 1.25 inches generally south and east of I-15. \nWe should continue to see decent CAPE values around 300-800 J/kg \nthis afternoon across Lincoln, Clark, Mohave, and eastern San \nBernardino counties with the higher terrain favored for any storm \ndevelopment, although storms will generally remain isolated. With \nthe fairly deep moisture still in place, some storms could produce\nflash flooding. As the upper level high builds through the week \nwe will see increasing warming aloft which will help to suppress \nthunderstorm development. Some isolated storms are possible again \nWednesday, but will generally become more isolated. Moisture will \ngradually decrease later in the week with dry southwesterly flow, \nreducing precipitation potential even further as we head into the \nlatter part of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast \nPackage...Typical light wind patterns are expected through the \nperiod as east winds become southwest tonight with speeds under 8KT. \nLight and variable winds Wednesday morning will become east again \nWednesday afternoon. Isolated showers and thunderstorms are possible \nin the terrain of the Southern Great Basin and western Arizona this \nafternoon then again Wednesday afternoon, but in general it will \nremain dry and impacts from precipitation are not expected in or \naround the terminals. Temperatures will exceed 100F through 04Z this \nevening then again 17Z-04Z tomorrow. VFR skies prevailing. \n  \nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Typical daytime and \nnighttime wind patterns with winds generally under 10KT are expected \ntoday and tonight. KDAG will briefly gust out of the west to around \n20KT this evening like they typically do, but gusts should end by \n09Z tonight. Isolated showers and thunderstorms are possible in the \nterrain of the Southern Great Basin and western Arizona this \nafternoon, diminishing after sunset. VFR skies prevailing through \nthe period.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           TUE, AUG 25 WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     111(2011)   109(2001)*  110(2017)*  110(2019)*\nBishop        103(2020)   103(2019)*  103(2019)*  103(2017)\nNeedles       119(1924)   122(1924)   120(1891)   116(2023)*\nDaggett       111(1985)   111(2011)   111(2017)   111(2017)\nKingman       106(2020)   106(2020)*  107(2017)*  107(2009)\nDesert Rock   107(1985)   106(2001)*  107(2017)   107(2017)\nDeath Valley  123(2011)   122(2017)*  124(1924)   120(2011)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      TUE, AUG 25 WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     87(2020)    88(2020)*   87(2011)*   86(2019)* \nBishop        65(1988)    66(1988)    67(1970)    62(1970) \nNeedles       92(2020)*   93(2011)    93(2011)    94(2017) \nDaggett       81(2003)*   82(1981)*   81(2011)*   82(2017)* \nKingman       78(2011)    79(1945)    79(2020)*   75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   78(1998)*   78(1985)*   78(1995)*   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley  98(1991)*   98(1991)*   95(2011)*  100(2019)* \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow \nAVIATION...Nickerson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
